# DE asset workflow

Use the existing DE visual system; do not create a competing asset system.

## Source preference
For new externally sourced content, prefer in this order when a suitable licensed asset is available:
1. Adobe Stock Free
2. Unsplash
3. Motion Array Free
4. Pixabay
5. Vecteezy or Freepik only after verifying the exact asset license and attribution requirement.

Never assume an asset is free merely because it can be previewed or downloaded. Never use watermarked previews, scraped copies, search-engine thumbnails, competitor-owned imagery, or assets with unknown provenance.

## Brand gate
Before using any candidate, read `design/IMAGERY.md`, `.cursor/rules/brand.mdc`, and relevant page-specific rules. Existing Blog and Store color locks remain authoritative.

Reject generic hacker hoodies, glowing padlocks/shields, random laptops, robots, binary-code wallpaper, cyberpunk/neon grids, rainbow gradients, stock-photo handshakes, or imagery that reads as generic AI cyber art.

Prefer authentic enterprise technology, infrastructure, business environments, Arizona/Phoenix context where relevant, restrained abstract technology, editorial photography, and high-quality motion that supports rather than competes with typography.

## Intake and provenance
Every external candidate must have a source note under `design/assets/source-notes/` using `TEMPLATE.md` before it is approved for production use. Record provider, exact source URL, asset/author ID where available, license class, commercial-use status, attribution requirement, model/property-release concerns, download date, original filename, intended placement, and reviewer notes.

If licensing is uncertain, mark the asset blocked and do not ship it.

## File handling
- Raw/source/licensed originals belong in the designated non-public source location, never directly under `client/public`.
- Do not overwrite originals.
- Public files must be optimized derivatives only.
- Prefer WebP/AVIF for photographic stills when supported; preserve PNG only when alpha is required. Use JPEG only when it is the best photographic fallback.
- For motion, ship only the smallest production-appropriate derivative and provide a reduced-motion/static fallback when the placement animates.
- Use descriptive kebab-case filenames that describe concept/placement, not provider download IDs alone.

## Approval flow
`inbox -> review -> approved source -> optimized public derivative -> registry -> rendered QA`

A downloaded asset is not automatically approved. Approval requires brand fit, licensing/provenance, crop/responsive usability, performance suitability, and placement QA.

## Public registry
When an external still is actually used on the public site, register it in `client/src/lib/visualAssets.ts` rather than scattering unexplained hard-coded asset paths. Extend the source union/type deliberately if a new provider is introduced.

Do not re-enable retired Meshy/sculpture assets just because they exist in inventory.

## Placement QA
Verify the real rendered placement at 390, 768, and 1440 when the asset is materially visible. Check focal crop, text contrast, CLS/layout stability, loading behavior, alt text, reduced motion, and whether the image still looks intentional beside neighboring sections.

## Stop conditions
Do not ship an asset when any of these are unresolved:
- license/provenance is unknown;
- required attribution is missing;
- recognizable trademark/person/property creates an unresolved endorsement/release risk;
- only a watermarked or preview version is available;
- image quality is insufficient at rendered size;
- the asset conflicts with `design/IMAGERY.md` or page-specific design locks.
